lip, but the newest high-quality surveys split between a small Jackson lead and a small Collins lead. A modest Democratic edge of about two points with wide uncertainty best reflects that genuinely close race.", "watch": "A run of consistent high-quality polls in either direction, or movement tied to the Supreme Court issue, could quickly shift this between Lean D and Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race is one of the closest contests of the cycle, with four-term Republican incumbent Susan Collins defending her seat against Democratic state House Speaker Troy Jackson. Recent polls are tightly clustered: several show Jackson narrowly ahead, while others, including a New York Times/Siena survey, give Collins a small edge. Averages across aggregators put Jackson up roughly one to two points.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race a Tossup, though four of ten rate it Lean D for a flip, and prediction markets price Jackson's chances near 60 percent. Collins holds a large fundraising advantage and her incumbency historically outperforms, but the political environment and lingering fallout from her Supreme Court vote weigh on her. The race sits on a knife's edge, tilting slightly Democratic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}]}